      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "iterative is the lowest-area family (shared datapath, no pipeline registers). It is the only family that can plausibly minimise luts_plus_ffs while meeting max_abs_err <= 2^-10. data_width 12-16 and n_iter 10-16 cover the accuracy sweet spot: W=12 with N~12-14 typically gives ~10-12 bits accuracy; W=16 with N~10-12 gives higher accuracy at modest area cost. angle_guard -1..1 and frac_guard 0..1 keep the datapath narrow. Both rounding modes are explored since round can buy ~0.5 bit accuracy for negligible area."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -1,
          "high": 1
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 1
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2..4 can hit >=1 MSPS throughput at moderate Fmax while keeping area close to iterative (shared FSM, no per-stage pipeline registers). k=2 halves latency vs iterative; k=3-4 further reduce latency at the cost of more parallel adders/shifters. The same accuracy parameters (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) apply, so the accuracy-vs-area trade-off is identical to iterative; the k parameter trades area for throughput/latency. This family maps the throughput-vs-area portion of the Pareto front that iterative cannot reach (iterative at 100 MHz gives ~0.03 MSPS, far below 1 MSPS, so iterative designs will be infeasible on throughput unless Fmax is very high — but unrolled_k with small k can meet 1 MSPS at realistic Fmax)."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts_plus_ffs) as the primary objective. Among my assigned families (iterative, unrolled_k), iterative is the absolute minimum-area option but produces only 1 result every N+3 cycles, so at a realistic Fmax (~100-200 MHz) its throughput is ~0.02-0.06 MSPS — far below the 1 MSPS constraint. Thus iterative designs will likely be infeasible on throughput, but I still allocate 50% budget to them because: (1) if Fmax is high enough (e.g., >300 MHz with small N), iterative could barely meet throughput, and (2) the accuracy-vs-area trade-off is identical across families, so iterative results anchor the accuracy side of the front. unrolled_k with k=2-4 is the realistic path to >=1 MSPS: at Fmax ~100-200 MHz, k=2 gives 2 results/cycle → 200-400 MSPS, easily meeting the constraint, while area is only modestly above iterative. I focus data_width 12-16 and n_iter 10-16 because CORDIC accuracy is roughly min(W, N) bits; W=12, N=12 gives ~10-11 bits, and W=16, N=12 gives ~12 bits, bracketing the 2^-10 requirement. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0-1) to minimise area since the accuracy budget is already met by W and N. Both rounding modes are explored to capture the free accuracy gain from rounding."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and meets all constraints. The minimum area design (iterative, 257 LUTs+FFs) achieves 10.3 bits accuracy (max_abs_err 0.00081 < 0.000976562) and 11 MSPS throughput (> 1 MSPS). The front extends to 11.8 bits accuracy with 411 LUTs+FFs, covering the trade-off space effectively. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum luts_plus_ffs (257) as per the selection rule."
,
